Pixel 9a GNSS SPI bring-up scripts (MODEM-PLAN G3, 2026-10-28/29)
=================================================================

Bring-up only, not for merge. The scripts that ran the tries of
MODEM-PLAN G3 (derekross/omarchy-mobile, device/tegu/MODEM-PLAN.md) on
the palmtop and the offline checks of their captures, published so the
plan can link each try to what ran. Paths are the palmtop's and the
laptop's as they were.

On the palmtop (/usr/local/bin):

  g7-strace.sh   fifth try onwards: start the modem with GNSS, attach
                 strace to gnssd, request a location, record 240 s of
                 syscalls and gnssd's log under /var/log/omamobile/g7.
  g7-min         the morning's short sessions (installed as g7-short):
                 engine start, 35 s of frames dumped by the gnssif
                 built with GNSS_PKT_DEBUG, then stop, so Kepler's
                 watchdog resets do not pile up; rx.txt holds the bytes.

On the laptop, one case per fresh boot:

  run-case.sh NAME "ARGS"    waits for the palmtop, runs g7-strace under
                             systemd-run with GNSS_SPI_ARGS=ARGS (the
                             device package's omamobile-tegu-modem-up
                             appends them to insmod gnss_spi.ko), then
                             counts bad checksums, ACK timeouts, watchdog
                             and hard-fault lines in gnssd's log.
  run-short.sh NAME "ARGS"   the same with g7-short.

Offline, on a saved strace (reads of /dev/gnss_ipc) or rx.txt:

  chpp.py       CHPP frames in the reads and whether their CRC32 (over
                the 8-byte header and payload) matches the footer.
  realign.py    valid frames per left shift of 0-7 bits (the one-bit-late
                finding of the twenty-ninth try).
  realign2.py   valid and invalid frames by length after a one-bit shift,
                whole buffer and per 64-byte read.
  bits.py       at which bit offset modulo 8 the CHPP preamble arrives.
  frames.py     the first N reads that hold a preamble, as hex.
  frames_dump.py  the same CRC check on the kernel-log dump of g7-min.
  brute.py      set the dropped bit at every 64-byte read boundary of a
                long frame every possible way and see if any passes.
  diffpos.py    where copies of a resent frame disagree, by offset in
                the 64-byte read.
  long.py       the first three reads of 192 bytes or more, in 64-byte
                rows.
  xor.py        footer against computed CRC of the "BAD checksum" lines.

g7-short: the palmtop's own copy (recovered 2026-10-29): the fifth try's
240 s strace session, output in /var/log/omamobile/g7/, which is what
run-short.sh counts. g7-min is the later 12 s session with its own
directory; the two are different scripts.
